WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.635
Use-after-free prevention starts with ownership.

2
00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:10.163
An owner controls an object's lifetime: it creates the object, decides when lifetime ends, and is responsible for cleanup.

3
00:00:10.163 --> 00:00:13.551
Borrowed access temporarily uses an object owned somewhere else.

4
00:00:13.551 --> 00:00:16.939
Observation refers to an object without controlling its lifetime.

5
00:00:16.939 --> 00:00:20.327
Ownership transfer moves responsibility from one owner to another.

6
00:00:20.327 --> 00:00:23.715
These roles need to be visible in the design.

7
00:00:23.715 --> 00:00:28.985
When a borrower stores access longer than the owner expects, the design becomes fragile.

8
00:00:28.985 --> 00:00:37.642
The original call may be safe, but the stored pointer, reference, iterator, or view can become stale after destruction, reset, movement, or container

9
00:00:37.642 --> 00:00:38.019
modification.

10
00:00:38.019 --> 00:00:46.677
That is why lifetime contracts should appear in API signatures, class members, factory functions, and code review notes rather than only in tribal

11
00:00:46.677 --> 00:00:47.053
memory.

12
00:00:47.053 --> 00:00:50.064
Modern C++ gives useful vocabulary for these contracts.

13
00:00:50.064 --> 00:00:52.699
A value member usually means direct ownership.

14
00:00:52.699 --> 00:00:55.334
A std::unique_ptr communicates exclusive dynamic ownership.

15
00:00:55.334 --> 00:00:57.969
A std::shared_ptr communicates intentional shared lifetime.

16
00:00:57.969 --> 00:01:02.110
A std::weak_ptr observes a shared object without keeping it alive.

17
00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:08.133
References and raw pointers can represent borrowed access when their lifetime rules are clear and local.

18
00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:11.144
The most helpful designs make destruction timing unsurprising.

19
00:01:11.144 --> 00:01:16.790
Constructors establish valid ownership, destructors release resources, and moves leave old objects in documented states.

20
00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:21.684
Factory functions should return owning values or smart pointers when they create resources.

21
00:01:21.684 --> 00:01:26.577
Member variables should not hide whether they own or merely observe another object.

22
00:01:26.577 --> 00:01:32.977
The fewer hidden lifetime agreements a reader must remember, the less room there is for stale access.

23
00:01:32.977 --> 00:01:41.634
Reviewers should ask: who owns this object, when can it be destroyed, who may borrow it, and can any access outlive the owner?

24
00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:46.904
If those answers are hard to find, the code is already difficult to trust.

25
00:01:46.904 --> 00:01:53.680
Clear ownership does not eliminate every bug, but it removes the ambiguity that allows stale access to hide.
